 "drivingQuestion": "What does the mishna's clause 'only those fit to testify' exclude?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "two answers stand; relation explained in next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.31a.14",
   "daf": "31a",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "אֵינָהּ נוֹהֶגֶת אֶלָּא בִּרְאוּיִן לְהָעִיד כּוּ׳. לְאַפּוֹקֵי מַאי? אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: לְאַפּוֹקֵי מֶלֶךְ. וְרַב אַחָא בַּר יַעֲקֹב אָמַר: לְאַפּוֹקֵי מְשַׂחֵק בְּקוּבְיָא.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that the oath of testimony «is practiced only with regard to» those «fit to testify.» The Gemara asks: This statement serves «to exclude what» person not allowed to testify who was not mentioned explicitly in the mishna? «Rav Pappa said:» It serves «to exclude a king» who, although he is neither a relative nor otherwise disqualified from testifying, does not testify in court. «And Rav Aḥa bar Ya’akov said:» It serves «to exclude one who plays with dice,» who is disqualified as a witness by rabbinic law."
  }
 ],
